// Word data the T9 game draws from: English words, length 4-8.
//
// Built in layers:
// 1. Start from a broad dictionary word list (words_alpha), filtered to
//    words that are real per WordNet's lemma list (excluding WordNet's
//    'instance' senses — specific named people/places like "Comte" or
//    "Aaron" — so those are excluded while dual-use words like "hammer" or
//    "Iranian" are kept) or a regular inflection of one (stripping a
//    regular -s/-es/-ed/-ing/-er/-est suffix, handling e-drop and doubled
//    consonants), plus a small hand-added list of function words and
//    irregular verb/plural forms WordNet does not index at all (else,
//    could, went, were, women, isle, fled, ...).
// 2. Keep only words where the word ITSELF, or the base it stems from, is
//    common enough to appear in the top 30,000 of a frequency-ranked word
//    list (out of a fuller 50k list we have on hand — this is just where we
//    cut it, not a separate dataset) — e.g. "repel" passes at 30k (rank
//    21,530) despite narrowly missing a 20k cutoff, while "lamer" passes
//    via its base "lame" regardless of the cutoff. Deliberately looser than
//    gating on the inflected surface form directly (which rejected
//    legitimate words like "lamer"), tightened from an initial 50k cutoff
//    to control obscurity/difficulty, then loosened from 20k to 30k after
//    20k rejected "repel" despite it being a common word.
// 3. A manual pass over every word step 1-2 added to WORDS_GOOD, reading
//    each one's actual WordNet definition, removing ones that looked
//    wrong — double inflections ("aboded"), misspellings ("chaped" for
//    "chapped"), nonstandard comparatives ("wronger"), overly obscure/
//    archaic/dialectal terms ("bedlamer", "gentles"), proper nouns/ethnic
//    group names used as generic plurals ("aztecs", "mongols"), and
//    offensive terms (slurs and other inappropriate words, e.g. "dagos",
//    "injun", "squaw") — plus a lighter pattern-based safety scan (not an
//    exhaustive manual review) over the much larger set of newly-added
//    real-but-not-good words each time the cutoff widened, catching things
//    like "fakeer" and a few slurs. See exclude_good.txt /
//    exclude_dict_only.txt in the scratchpad this was built from for the
//    exact lists (both get removed from DICTIONARY; exclude_good.txt
//    additionally guarantees removal from WORDS_GOOD specifically).
//
// DICTIONARY: the full list, used to check whether a typed guess is a real
// word at all (hangman mode).
// WORDS_GOOD: the subset that is also 'good' under the current t9-logic.js
// rules (including wraparound merges, e.g. HINDU), precomputed offline —
// classifying the whole dictionary against every possible circular
// digit-merge takes several seconds, too slow to redo on every page load —
// rather than at runtime. Whenever the good/bad rules in t9-logic.js
// change, or the dictionary itself is regenerated, this needs regenerating
// to match — see the isGoodWord-based filter used to build it.
// WORDS_GOOD_RANK[i] is WORDS_GOOD[i]'s frequency rank (1 = most common,
// out of the fuller 50k-word frequency list, kept for finer-grained
// difficulty gauging even though the gate above only checks the top 30k) —
// its own rank if it has one, else its base stem's rank. Used to gauge a
// hangman puzzle's difficulty from how rare its rarest valid answer is.
